1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,030
It's the spring of nineteen fifty-six.

2
00:00:03,210 --> 00:00:05,102
You're twenty-seven years old.

3
00:00:05,282 --> 00:00:07,409
You've been married for four years,

4
00:00:07,409 --> 00:00:09,171
and you have two little kids.

5
00:00:09,351 --> 00:00:10,781
For the last few years,

6
00:00:10,781 --> 00:00:13,329
you've been living in a cramped apartment

7
00:00:13,329 --> 00:00:14,448
over a shoe store,

8
00:00:14,448 --> 00:00:16,561
or in your in-laws' spare bedroom.

9
00:00:16,741 --> 00:00:17,755
And this Sunday,

10
00:00:17,755 --> 00:00:19,657
you and your wife are going to

11
00:00:19,657 --> 00:00:21,432
drive out to look at houses.

12
00:00:21,612 --> 00:00:23,330
Your very first house.

13
00:00:23,679 --> 00:00:26,791
Millions of young Americans made that same

14
00:00:26,791 --> 00:00:28,347
drive in the fifties.

15
00:00:28,527 --> 00:00:31,402
Between nineteen forty and nineteen sixty,

16
00:00:31,402 --> 00:00:34,141
the share of American families who owned

17
00:00:34,141 --> 00:00:37,359
their home jumped from about forty-four percent

18
00:00:37,359 --> 00:00:39,208
to about sixty-two percent.

19
00:00:39,388 --> 00:00:41,443
It was one of the biggest changes

20
00:00:41,443 --> 00:00:42,501
in American life,

21
00:00:42,501 --> 00:00:44,868
and it happened in about twenty years.

22
00:00:45,218 --> 00:00:47,231
So what did it actually cost to

23
00:00:47,231 --> 00:00:50,024
buy your first house in nineteen fifty-six?

24
00:00:50,204 --> 00:00:51,226
How did you pay for it?

25
00:00:51,406 --> 00:00:52,230
What did you get?

26
00:00:52,410 --> 00:00:53,989
And how does it compare with today?

27
00:00:54,169 --> 00:00:55,468
Let's walk through it,

28
00:00:55,468 --> 00:00:57,416
from the model home to the moving

29
00:00:57,416 --> 00:00:57,652
van.

30
00:00:58,001 --> 00:00:59,964
First, a little background.

31
00:01:00,144 --> 00:01:03,232
During the Great Depression and the Second

32
00:01:03,232 --> 00:01:05,879
World War, almost no new houses were

33
00:01:05,879 --> 00:01:07,864
built for regular families.

34
00:01:08,044 --> 00:01:10,748
When the soldiers came home in nineteen

35
00:01:10,748 --> 00:01:12,481
forty-five and forty-six,

36
00:01:12,481 --> 00:01:15,115
there was a terrible housing shortage.

37
00:01:15,295 --> 00:01:17,991
Young couples lived in basements,

38
00:01:17,991 --> 00:01:21,912
attics, garages, trailers and old army barracks.

39
00:01:22,092 --> 00:01:24,373
Millions of them were desperate for a

40
00:01:24,373 --> 00:01:25,482
home of their own.

41
00:01:25,833 --> 00:01:27,293
On Sunday afternoons,

42
00:01:27,293 --> 00:01:29,657
young couples drove out to the new

43
00:01:29,657 --> 00:01:31,951
developments at the edge of town.

44
00:01:32,131 --> 00:01:34,248
Builders put up a model home,

45
00:01:34,248 --> 00:01:36,000
furnished and decorated,

46
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,482
with flags and balloons out front.

47
00:01:38,662 --> 00:01:41,114
There might be free coffee and doughnuts,

48
00:01:41,114 --> 00:01:43,028
and a salesman in a suit waiting

49
00:01:43,028 --> 00:01:44,165
to show you around.

50
00:01:44,515 --> 00:01:46,082
The houses were simple.

51
00:01:46,262 --> 00:01:48,761
A ranch house or a Cape Cod,

52
00:01:48,761 --> 00:01:50,368
usually one story,

53
00:01:50,368 --> 00:01:51,707
on a small lot.

54
00:01:51,887 --> 00:01:53,624
Two or three bedrooms,

55
00:01:53,624 --> 00:01:55,836
one bathroom, a living room,

56
00:01:55,836 --> 00:01:58,205
and a kitchen big enough for a

57
00:01:58,205 --> 00:01:58,679
table.

58
00:01:58,859 --> 00:02:00,149
In nineteen fifty,

59
00:02:00,149 --> 00:02:03,304
the average new single-family house was only

60
00:02:03,304 --> 00:02:05,384
about a thousand square feet.

61
00:02:05,564 --> 00:02:06,684
By the late fifties,

62
00:02:06,684 --> 00:02:08,420
it was getting a little bigger.

63
00:02:08,770 --> 00:02:10,609
But to a young couple who had

64
00:02:10,609 --> 00:02:12,321
grown up in the Depression,

65
00:02:12,321 --> 00:02:13,843
it looked like a palace.

66
00:02:14,023 --> 00:02:17,169
A brand-new kitchen with modern appliances.

67
00:02:17,349 --> 00:02:18,928
A yard for the kids.

68
00:02:19,108 --> 00:02:20,118
A driveway.

69
00:02:20,298 --> 00:02:21,169
Closets.

70
00:02:21,349 --> 00:02:23,776
A house that nobody else had ever

71
00:02:23,776 --> 00:02:24,437
lived in.

72
00:02:24,617 --> 00:02:27,460
Many couples signed the papers that same

73
00:02:27,460 --> 00:02:28,170
afternoon.

74
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:31,178
Builders had learned to build fast.

75
00:02:31,358 --> 00:02:34,460
William Levitt, the most famous of them,

76
00:02:34,460 --> 00:02:37,175
built houses like an assembly line.

77
00:02:37,355 --> 00:02:40,016
Trucks dropped materials at each lot,

78
00:02:40,016 --> 00:02:42,676
and teams of workers moved from house

79
00:02:42,676 --> 00:02:44,762
to house, each doing one job.

80
00:02:44,942 --> 00:02:45,929
At the peak,

81
00:02:45,929 --> 00:02:47,822
his crews in Levittown,

82
00:02:47,822 --> 00:02:51,196
New York, finished around thirty houses a

83
00:02:51,196 --> 00:02:51,525
day.

84
00:02:51,875 --> 00:02:53,535
So what was the price?

85
00:02:53,715 --> 00:02:55,541
In nineteen fifty-six,

86
00:02:55,541 --> 00:02:58,363
a basic new house in a development

87
00:02:58,363 --> 00:03:01,932
might cost between ten and fifteen thousand

88
00:03:01,932 --> 00:03:02,596
dollars.

89
00:03:02,776 --> 00:03:04,793
The median value of a home in

90
00:03:04,793 --> 00:03:07,714
the nineteen sixty census was about eleven

91
00:03:07,714 --> 00:03:09,800
thousand nine hundred dollars.

92
00:03:09,980 --> 00:03:12,625
Some small houses sold for less than

93
00:03:12,625 --> 00:03:13,580
ten thousand.

94
00:03:13,930 --> 00:03:16,135
That sounds unbelievably cheap today.

95
00:03:16,315 --> 00:03:17,929
But let's put it in perspective.

96
00:03:18,109 --> 00:03:19,653
In nineteen fifty-six,

97
00:03:19,653 --> 00:03:22,391
the median family income in America was

98
00:03:22,391 --> 00:03:25,409
about four thousand eight hundred dollars a

99
00:03:25,409 --> 00:03:25,760
year.

100
00:03:25,940 --> 00:03:28,330
So a typical house cost about two

101
00:03:28,330 --> 00:03:30,503
and a half times what a family

102
00:03:30,503 --> 00:03:31,734
earned in a year.

103
00:03:32,084 --> 00:03:35,196
Today, the median house price in America

104
00:03:35,196 --> 00:03:37,608
is around five to six times the

105
00:03:37,608 --> 00:03:39,475
median household income,

106
00:03:39,475 --> 00:03:41,731
and in many cities much more.

107
00:03:41,911 --> 00:03:43,479
That's the real difference.

108
00:03:43,659 --> 00:03:45,643
It's not just that the numbers were

109
00:03:45,643 --> 00:03:46,097
smaller.

110
00:03:46,277 --> 00:03:49,107
A house in nineteen fifty-six cost far

111
00:03:49,107 --> 00:03:52,012
less, compared with what people earned.

112
00:03:52,363 --> 00:03:54,262
Few families could pay cash,

113
00:03:54,262 --> 00:03:55,822
so they got a mortgage.

114
00:03:56,002 --> 00:03:58,209
And here, the government had made a

115
00:03:58,209 --> 00:03:59,218
huge difference.

116
00:03:59,398 --> 00:04:01,532
Before the nineteen thirties,

117
00:04:01,532 --> 00:04:04,033
a home loan usually lasted five to

118
00:04:04,033 --> 00:04:06,387
ten years, and you might need to

119
00:04:06,387 --> 00:04:08,152
put down half the price.

120
00:04:08,332 --> 00:04:10,570
Many loans ended with a big payment

121
00:04:10,570 --> 00:04:12,361
that families couldn't make.

122
00:04:12,711 --> 00:04:15,153
The Federal Housing Administration,

123
00:04:15,153 --> 00:04:17,387
created in nineteen thirty-four,

124
00:04:17,387 --> 00:04:18,574
changed all that.

125
00:04:18,754 --> 00:04:21,895
It insured long-term mortgages of twenty or

126
00:04:21,895 --> 00:04:24,744
thirty years, with small down payments.

127
00:04:24,924 --> 00:04:27,842
Suddenly, a monthly payment could be about

128
00:04:27,842 --> 00:04:29,023
the same as rent.

129
00:04:29,203 --> 00:04:30,929
Banks were willing to lend,

130
00:04:30,929 --> 00:04:33,870
because the government stood behind the loans.

131
00:04:34,220 --> 00:04:35,380
And for veterans,

132
00:04:35,380 --> 00:04:36,472
there was the G.

133
00:04:36,652 --> 00:04:37,012
I.

134
00:04:37,192 --> 00:04:37,505
Bill.

135
00:04:37,685 --> 00:04:39,931
Signed in nineteen forty-four,

136
00:04:39,931 --> 00:04:43,523
it guaranteed home loans for returning soldiers,

137
00:04:43,523 --> 00:04:46,068
often with no down payment at all.

138
00:04:46,248 --> 00:04:47,602
By the mid-fifties,

139
00:04:47,602 --> 00:04:50,097
millions of veterans had used it to

140
00:04:50,097 --> 00:04:50,810
buy homes.

141
00:04:50,990 --> 00:04:52,966
Many young men who had grown up

142
00:04:52,966 --> 00:04:55,834
in rented rooms became homeowners before they

143
00:04:55,834 --> 00:04:56,726
turned thirty.

144
00:04:57,076 --> 00:04:59,753
Mortgage interest rates in the mid-fifties were

145
00:04:59,753 --> 00:05:01,461
around four and a half to five

146
00:05:01,461 --> 00:05:01,917
percent.

147
00:05:02,097 --> 00:05:04,522
On a twelve thousand dollar house,

148
00:05:04,522 --> 00:05:06,733
with a small down payment and a

149
00:05:06,733 --> 00:05:10,013
thirty-year loan, the monthly payment might be

150
00:05:10,013 --> 00:05:12,295
around sixty to seventy dollars,

151
00:05:12,295 --> 00:05:14,078
plus taxes and insurance.

152
00:05:14,258 --> 00:05:15,409
For many families,

153
00:05:15,409 --> 00:05:17,775
that was less than they'd been paying

154
00:05:17,775 --> 00:05:18,287
in rent.

155
00:05:18,637 --> 00:05:20,494
Getting the loan was personal.

156
00:05:20,674 --> 00:05:22,726
You sat across a desk from a

157
00:05:22,726 --> 00:05:25,145
loan officer at the local bank or

158
00:05:25,145 --> 00:05:26,391
savings and loan,

159
00:05:26,391 --> 00:05:27,710
in your best suit.

160
00:05:27,890 --> 00:05:29,439
He asked about your job,

161
00:05:29,439 --> 00:05:30,923
how long you'd held it,

162
00:05:30,923 --> 00:05:32,859
and whether you had any debts.

163
00:05:33,039 --> 00:05:35,176
A steady job at the plant and

164
00:05:35,176 --> 00:05:37,902
a good reputation in town counted for

165
00:05:37,902 --> 00:05:38,345
a lot.

166
00:05:38,525 --> 00:05:41,057
The whole thing was done on paper,

167
00:05:41,057 --> 00:05:42,472
with carbon copies.

168
00:05:42,822 --> 00:05:44,923
But not everyone could get that deal.

169
00:05:45,103 --> 00:05:48,803
For decades, federal housing agencies and banks

170
00:05:48,803 --> 00:05:52,109
refused to insure or lend in neighborhoods

171
00:05:52,109 --> 00:05:54,235
where Black families lived,

172
00:05:54,235 --> 00:05:56,597
a practice known as redlining.

173
00:05:56,777 --> 00:05:58,443
Many new developments,

174
00:05:58,443 --> 00:06:01,926
including Levittown, had rules that kept Black

175
00:06:01,926 --> 00:06:03,592
families out entirely.

176
00:06:03,942 --> 00:06:07,015
The Supreme Court ruled in nineteen forty-eight

177
00:06:07,015 --> 00:06:09,697
that those racial rules in property deeds

178
00:06:09,697 --> 00:06:11,790
could not be enforced by courts.

179
00:06:11,970 --> 00:06:15,363
But discrimination in housing continued for years,

180
00:06:15,363 --> 00:06:17,874
and it wasn't outlawed until the Fair

181
00:06:17,874 --> 00:06:20,317
Housing Act of nineteen sixty-eight.

182
00:06:20,497 --> 00:06:22,185
For millions of families,

183
00:06:22,185 --> 00:06:24,142
the dream of a first house in

184
00:06:24,142 --> 00:06:26,302
the fifties stayed out of reach.

185
00:06:26,653 --> 00:06:28,611
For the families who did buy,

186
00:06:28,611 --> 00:06:30,704
moving day was a big adventure.

187
00:06:30,884 --> 00:06:33,405
Many couldn't afford a moving company,

188
00:06:33,405 --> 00:06:35,462
so they borrowed a truck from a

189
00:06:35,462 --> 00:06:38,182
friend, or loaded everything into the car

190
00:06:38,182 --> 00:06:39,708
and made a dozen trips.

191
00:06:39,888 --> 00:06:42,655
Brothers, cousins and friends from work helped

192
00:06:42,655 --> 00:06:43,859
carry the furniture.

193
00:06:44,209 --> 00:06:46,520
And there often wasn't much furniture to

194
00:06:46,520 --> 00:06:46,809
move.

195
00:06:46,989 --> 00:06:49,724
A bed, a kitchen table and chairs,

196
00:06:49,724 --> 00:06:51,655
a couch from a relative,

197
00:06:51,655 --> 00:06:53,746
and a few boxes of dishes.

198
00:06:53,926 --> 00:06:56,910
Many new homeowners had empty rooms for

199
00:06:56,910 --> 00:06:58,593
months, or even years.

200
00:06:58,773 --> 00:07:01,216
Living rooms with only a couch and

201
00:07:01,216 --> 00:07:03,659
a television, and a bedroom with a

202
00:07:03,659 --> 00:07:05,240
mattress on the floor.

203
00:07:05,591 --> 00:07:07,626
The first night in the new house

204
00:07:07,626 --> 00:07:08,772
was unforgettable.

205
00:07:08,952 --> 00:07:11,449
Children ran from room to room,

206
00:07:11,449 --> 00:07:12,899
claiming bedrooms.

207
00:07:13,079 --> 00:07:15,104
Parents sat on the front steps,

208
00:07:15,104 --> 00:07:16,802
looking out at the street,

209
00:07:16,802 --> 00:07:18,826
hardly believing it was theirs.

210
00:07:19,006 --> 00:07:22,150
Some couples remember eating sandwiches on the

211
00:07:22,150 --> 00:07:25,362
kitchen floor, because the table hadn't arrived

212
00:07:25,362 --> 00:07:25,635
yet.

213
00:07:25,815 --> 00:07:27,493
And many say it was one of

214
00:07:27,493 --> 00:07:29,751
the happiest nights of their lives.

215
00:07:30,101 --> 00:07:32,608
The neighborhood was often brand new too.

216
00:07:32,788 --> 00:07:34,901
Mud instead of grass.

217
00:07:35,081 --> 00:07:37,786
Little sticks where trees would someday be.

218
00:07:37,966 --> 00:07:39,568
No sidewalks yet,

219
00:07:39,568 --> 00:07:42,111
sometimes no paved streets.

220
00:07:42,291 --> 00:07:44,860
But all around you were young couples

221
00:07:44,860 --> 00:07:45,832
just like you,

222
00:07:45,832 --> 00:07:47,568
moving in the same month,

223
00:07:47,568 --> 00:07:49,164
with kids the same age.

224
00:07:49,515 --> 00:07:51,002
Once you had the house,

225
00:07:51,002 --> 00:07:52,231
you had to fill it.

226
00:07:52,411 --> 00:07:54,757
And the American economy was ready.

227
00:07:54,937 --> 00:07:57,319
Families bought refrigerators,

228
00:07:57,319 --> 00:08:01,291
washing machines, vacuum cleaners and televisions,

229
00:08:01,291 --> 00:08:03,435
often on installment plans.

230
00:08:03,615 --> 00:08:05,654
A few dollars down and a few

231
00:08:05,654 --> 00:08:06,819
dollars a month.

232
00:08:06,999 --> 00:08:10,497
Sears, Montgomery Ward and the local furniture

233
00:08:10,497 --> 00:08:12,398
store all offered credit.

234
00:08:12,748 --> 00:08:15,765
A new refrigerator in nineteen fifty-six might

235
00:08:15,765 --> 00:08:18,388
cost around two hundred to three hundred

236
00:08:18,388 --> 00:08:18,913
dollars.

237
00:08:19,093 --> 00:08:21,276
A television, about the same.

238
00:08:21,456 --> 00:08:22,849
A washing machine,

239
00:08:22,849 --> 00:08:24,242
maybe two hundred.

240
00:08:24,422 --> 00:08:26,448
Those were big purchases,

241
00:08:26,448 --> 00:08:28,475
a month or two of a man's

242
00:08:28,475 --> 00:08:28,799
pay.

243
00:08:28,979 --> 00:08:31,380
Many families bought one thing at a

244
00:08:31,380 --> 00:08:33,849
time, paying off the last one before

245
00:08:33,849 --> 00:08:34,946
buying the next.

246
00:08:35,296 --> 00:08:37,490
And many husbands became handymen.

247
00:08:37,670 --> 00:08:39,025
They built shelves,

248
00:08:39,025 --> 00:08:41,093
painted rooms, put up fences,

249
00:08:41,093 --> 00:08:42,662
finished the basement,

250
00:08:42,662 --> 00:08:45,159
and planted the lawn and the trees.

251
00:08:45,339 --> 00:08:47,057
Hardware stores boomed.

252
00:08:47,237 --> 00:08:50,116
Do-it-yourself became a national hobby.

253
00:08:50,296 --> 00:08:53,315
A man's Saturday belonged to the house.

254
00:08:53,665 --> 00:08:55,209
And then there was the lawn.

255
00:08:55,389 --> 00:08:56,446
In the fifties,

256
00:08:56,446 --> 00:08:58,489
the lawn became a symbol of a

257
00:08:58,489 --> 00:08:59,545
good homeowner.

258
00:08:59,725 --> 00:09:01,902
Men spent their weekends seeding,

259
00:09:01,902 --> 00:09:04,079
watering, fertilizing and mowing.

260
00:09:04,259 --> 00:09:07,653
Push mowers, and then gas-powered rotary mowers,

261
00:09:07,653 --> 00:09:10,128
buzzed up and down the street every

262
00:09:10,128 --> 00:09:11,330
Saturday morning.

263
00:09:11,510 --> 00:09:13,791
A brown, weedy lawn could get you

264
00:09:13,791 --> 00:09:16,003
a talking-to from the neighbors.

265
00:09:16,353 --> 00:09:17,990
What was it like to live there?

266
00:09:18,170 --> 00:09:19,737
For many families,

267
00:09:19,737 --> 00:09:20,608
wonderful.

268
00:09:20,788 --> 00:09:24,210
Children played in the street until dark,

269
00:09:24,210 --> 00:09:26,547
in packs of a dozen or more.

270
00:09:26,727 --> 00:09:28,840
Mothers met over the back fence.

271
00:09:29,020 --> 00:09:32,410
Neighbors had barbecues and cocktail parties.

272
00:09:32,590 --> 00:09:33,810
On summer nights,

273
00:09:33,810 --> 00:09:36,467
everyone sat out on their lawn chairs

274
00:09:36,467 --> 00:09:37,257
and talked.

275
00:09:37,607 --> 00:09:39,720
The new suburbs needed everything.

276
00:09:39,900 --> 00:09:42,132
New schools, new churches,

277
00:09:42,132 --> 00:09:43,935
new shopping centers,

278
00:09:43,935 --> 00:09:45,995
new parks and new roads.

279
00:09:46,175 --> 00:09:48,707
Many of the first homeowners joined the

280
00:09:48,707 --> 00:09:50,979
PTA, the church building committee,

281
00:09:50,979 --> 00:09:53,576
the Little League and the volunteer fire

282
00:09:53,576 --> 00:09:54,291
department.

283
00:09:54,471 --> 00:09:56,275
They built not just houses,

284
00:09:56,275 --> 00:09:57,745
but whole communities.

285
00:09:58,095 --> 00:09:59,569
There were problems too.

286
00:09:59,749 --> 00:10:01,974
Some of the houses were built very

287
00:10:01,974 --> 00:10:03,348
quickly, and cheaply.

288
00:10:03,528 --> 00:10:04,608
Basements leaked.

289
00:10:04,788 --> 00:10:05,763
Walls were thin.

290
00:10:05,943 --> 00:10:07,816
And for wives without a car,

291
00:10:07,816 --> 00:10:10,156
the new suburbs could feel very far

292
00:10:10,156 --> 00:10:11,226
from everything.

293
00:10:11,406 --> 00:10:13,287
But most families who bought in the

294
00:10:13,287 --> 00:10:14,738
fifties never regretted it.

295
00:10:15,088 --> 00:10:17,526
Taxes were another thing to plan for.

296
00:10:17,706 --> 00:10:20,670
Property taxes paid for the new schools,

297
00:10:20,670 --> 00:10:23,633
and as the suburbs filled with children,

298
00:10:23,633 --> 00:10:24,893
those taxes rose.

299
00:10:25,073 --> 00:10:28,737
Many families also paid for homeowners insurance,

300
00:10:28,737 --> 00:10:30,532
water and sewer service,

301
00:10:30,532 --> 00:10:33,374
and garbage pickup for the first time.

302
00:10:33,554 --> 00:10:35,977
Being a homeowner meant a whole new

303
00:10:35,977 --> 00:10:36,946
list of bills,

304
00:10:36,946 --> 00:10:38,954
kept in a shoebox or a little

305
00:10:38,954 --> 00:10:41,170
file box on the kitchen counter.

306
00:10:41,520 --> 00:10:43,482
And here's the remarkable part.

307
00:10:43,662 --> 00:10:44,820
For many families,

308
00:10:44,820 --> 00:10:47,459
that first house became the most valuable

309
00:10:47,459 --> 00:10:48,875
thing they ever owned.

310
00:10:49,055 --> 00:10:51,769
A house bought for twelve thousand dollars

311
00:10:51,769 --> 00:10:54,547
in nineteen fifty-six might be worth twenty

312
00:10:54,547 --> 00:10:56,485
or thirty times as much today.

313
00:10:56,665 --> 00:10:58,074
It paid for college,

314
00:10:58,074 --> 00:10:59,694
helped with retirement,

315
00:10:59,694 --> 00:11:01,948
and was passed down to children.

316
00:11:02,298 --> 00:11:04,275
Many of those families paid off their

317
00:11:04,275 --> 00:11:06,466
thirty-year mortgage in the mid-eighties.

318
00:11:06,646 --> 00:11:09,016
Some held mortgage-burning parties,

319
00:11:09,016 --> 00:11:12,064
literally setting the paid-off papers on fire

320
00:11:12,064 --> 00:11:13,554
in the backyard grill,

321
00:11:13,554 --> 00:11:15,992
with friends and neighbors cheering.

322
00:11:16,172 --> 00:11:17,844
It was the end of a long

323
00:11:17,844 --> 00:11:19,585
road, and a proud moment.

324
00:11:19,935 --> 00:11:22,226
And many of those houses are still

325
00:11:22,226 --> 00:11:23,035
there today.

326
00:11:23,215 --> 00:11:25,177
Levittown, New York.

327
00:11:25,357 --> 00:11:27,040
Lakewood, California.

328
00:11:27,220 --> 00:11:29,148
Park Forest, Illinois.

329
00:11:29,328 --> 00:11:32,590
Thousands of suburbs all over the country.

330
00:11:32,770 --> 00:11:34,719
The houses have been expanded,

331
00:11:34,719 --> 00:11:36,277
remodeled and repainted,

332
00:11:36,277 --> 00:11:38,356
but you can still see the simple

333
00:11:38,356 --> 00:11:39,980
fifties shape underneath.

334
00:11:40,330 --> 00:11:42,568
Compare that with buying a first home

335
00:11:42,568 --> 00:11:42,931
today.

336
00:11:43,111 --> 00:11:46,042
The average first-time buyer in America is

337
00:11:46,042 --> 00:11:47,856
now in their mid-thirties,

338
00:11:47,856 --> 00:11:49,461
older than ever before.

339
00:11:49,641 --> 00:11:51,298
In nineteen fifty-six,

340
00:11:51,298 --> 00:11:54,761
many first-time buyers were in their twenties.

341
00:11:54,941 --> 00:11:57,585
Today, a down payment alone can take

342
00:11:57,585 --> 00:11:58,613
years to save,

343
00:11:58,613 --> 00:12:00,009
and in many cities,

344
00:12:00,009 --> 00:12:02,873
a small starter house costs hundreds of

345
00:12:02,873 --> 00:12:04,415
thousands of dollars.

346
00:12:04,765 --> 00:12:07,062
That doesn't mean life in the fifties

347
00:12:07,062 --> 00:12:07,621
was easy.

348
00:12:07,801 --> 00:12:09,485
Families worked hard,

349
00:12:09,485 --> 00:12:12,131
saved hard, and did without a lot

350
00:12:12,131 --> 00:12:12,933
of things.

351
00:12:13,113 --> 00:12:15,485
But a young working family really could

352
00:12:15,485 --> 00:12:17,188
buy a house on one paycheck.

353
00:12:17,368 --> 00:12:19,582
It was one of the great achievements

354
00:12:19,582 --> 00:12:20,751
of postwar America,

355
00:12:20,751 --> 00:12:22,842
and many people today wish it were

356
00:12:22,842 --> 00:12:23,765
still possible.

357
00:12:24,115 --> 00:12:26,615
If your parents bought their first house

358
00:12:26,615 --> 00:12:27,553
in the fifties,

359
00:12:27,553 --> 00:12:29,490
you might remember the stories.

360
00:12:29,670 --> 00:12:30,959
The tiny kitchen.

361
00:12:31,139 --> 00:12:32,637
The empty living room.

362
00:12:32,817 --> 00:12:34,825
The day the grass finally grew.

363
00:12:35,005 --> 00:12:36,890
And maybe you remember the day they

364
00:12:36,890 --> 00:12:37,536
paid it off.

365
00:12:37,716 --> 00:12:39,307
It was more than a house.

366
00:12:39,487 --> 00:12:41,508
It was the American dream,

367
00:12:41,508 --> 00:12:42,285
made real.

368
00:12:42,635 --> 00:12:44,458
So tell us in the comments.

369
00:12:44,638 --> 00:12:46,866
When did your family buy its first

370
00:12:46,866 --> 00:12:48,701
house, and what did it cost?

371
00:12:48,881 --> 00:12:50,704
Do you remember the address?

372
00:12:50,884 --> 00:12:52,219
Is it still standing?

373
00:12:52,399 --> 00:12:53,874
We read every comment.

374
00:12:54,054 --> 00:12:57,600
Next time, we're counting twenty-five things every

375
00:12:57,600 --> 00:12:59,940
nineteen sixties living room had.

376
00:13:00,120 --> 00:13:01,741
Thank you for spending this time with

377
00:13:01,741 --> 00:13:01,873
me.
